    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the performance study of a well undergoing water coning, predicting the water breakthrough time is an important factor. The most general method used for such predictions is a finite difference computer model. Such a method is expensive and requires a lengthy preparation. For the case of lateral drive systems, two empirical correlations have been published to determine the water breakthrough time. They were derived on an entirely empirical basis and neglected an important factor which is the water zone thickness. Most important, these correlations give significantly differing predictions. In this work, a theoretical study of the problem is presented. Using the small perturbation theory, approximate equations for a water cone are derived for various reservoir conditions. All reservoir parameters, including the water zone thickness, are included in the equations. A technique is then presented which relates the theoretical results with the observed ones. Results that were previously used to develop the two different correlations could thus be regrouped in a single correlation. Graphs and equations are also provided to adapt the procedure to hand calculations. A computer model based on the potential theory formulation of the coning problem is then presented. This model follows the water cone evolution up to breakthrough. It was used to generate data in order to extend the range of the breakthrough time correlation. A correlation is also presented to predict the limiting water-oil ratio for lateral drive systems. For this, it was assumed that the steady-state potential distribution in each phase is the same as in an equivalent single phase system. Results predicted by the correlation are compared with the experimental data. The model is then modified by partially removing the assumption that the flux density at the well is uniform in each single phase system. Results predicted by this method are then compared with the experimental data.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
